---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import EmailCTA from '../components/EmailCTA.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="The Montana Backcountry Day-Pack Checklist — Spiked Adrenaline"
  description="The exact list we run before every pack-in. Weather, med kit, kill kit, and the pieces that save a trip — or a life. Field-proven, no filler."
>
  <section class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">The checklist</p>
    <h1>The Montana Backcountry Day-Pack Checklist</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This is the exact list we check before every pack-in. Print it, screenshot it, tape it
      to the inside of your pack lid. Every item earned its spot on a real trip — usually
      the hard way. Nothing on here is filler, and nothing is negotiable once you've needed
      it and not had it.
    </p>

    <div class="panel group">
      <h2>On your body — always</h2>
      <ul class="check">
        <li><strong>Neck knife.</strong> Always on. Not in the pack, not in a pocket — on a cord, against your chest. Here's the scenario nobody plans for: you set the pack down to glass, follow a blood trail two ridges over, and now it's getting dark and the pack is a half-mile of deadfall behind you. Everything you own is in that pack — except the one tool hanging around your neck. A blade you're wearing can cut cordage, make fire prep, dress game, and free you from whatever you've tangled with. The knife in the pack is a tool. The knife on your body is a policy.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="panel group">
      <h2>Weather</h2>
      <ul class="check">
        <li><strong>Rain gear — jacket and pants.</strong> Montana doesn't read the forecast. A bluebird morning can turn into sideways graupel by 2 p.m., and wet-plus-wind is how hypothermia starts in September, not January. The jacket is obvious; the pants are the part people skip and regret — soaked thighs pull heat out of you with every step.</li>
        <li><strong>Gaiters.</strong> Wet grass before sunrise will soak your boots from the top down in twenty minutes — no rain required. Scree fills them from above, snow from everywhere. Dry feet aren't comfort, they're mileage: wet socks end trips.</li>
        <li><strong>Pack rain cover.</strong> Your puffy and your sleeping layers are only insulation while they're dry. Down that's been rained on is dead weight with a zipper. The cover weighs a few ounces and protects the entire system that keeps you alive at 2 a.m.</li>
        <li><strong>Bow string rain cover.</strong> The one even experienced bowhunters forget. A soaked string and cables change how your bow shoots — right at the moment a season's worth of practice is on the line. You maintained that string all summer; don't let a squall retune your bow on the pack-in.</li>
        <li><strong>Tarp.</strong> The most versatile pound in the pack. Glassing shelter when the weather sits down on you. Shade over quarters on a warm afternoon. Ground cloth for the deboning job. Emergency bivy when the day goes long. No single item buys you more options per ounce.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="panel group">
      <h2>Med kit</h2>
      <ul class="check">
        <li><strong>Lighter.</strong> Fire is first aid in the backcountry. Warmth against shock and hypothermia, sterilization, signal, morale. If a wreck happens, the first prescription is usually "get warm and think."</li>
        <li><strong>Ointments.</strong> Antibiotic for the cuts you'll actually get, blister care for the hot spots you'll actually feel. Treat a hot spot at mile two and it's a footnote; ignore it and it's the whole story of your trip.</li>
        <li><strong>Pain meds.</strong> Not for comfort — for function. A rolled ankle five miles in doesn't need to feel good, it needs to walk out.</li>
        <li><strong>Bandages.</strong> Enough to close and cover, not a pharmacy. You're stabilizing for the walk out, not performing surgery.</li>
        <li><strong>Disinfectant.</strong> Backcountry cuts live in dirt, blood, and game bags. Clean it now or explain it to a doctor later.</li>
        <li><strong>Wool sock.</strong> The quiet genius of the kit. It's a pressure wrap, an improvised tourniquet, a mitten over a wrecked hand, and — worst case — a dry sock. One item, four jobs, zero extra weight. That's the standard everything in this pack has to meet.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="panel group">
      <h2>Light &amp; power</h2>
      <ul class="check">
        <li><strong>Backup light.</strong> The headlamp you're wearing doesn't count — batteries die in the dark, not at the trailhead. Deadfall, blowdown timber, and blood trails all happen after sunset, and doing any of them by phone light is how a long night becomes a dangerous one.</li>
        <li><strong>Power brick.</strong> Your phone is your GPS, your map, your camera, and your emergency line — and cold kills batteries fast. A dead phone turns "I know exactly where I am" into a guess. Keep the brick warm and the phone alive.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="panel group">
      <h2>Food &amp; water</h2>
      <ul class="check">
        <li><strong>Stove with one meal.</strong> One hot meal is the difference between a survival night and a story you'll tell later. If you're benighted, hot food resets your head, warms your core, and buys you the judgment to make good decisions at midnight.</li>
        <li><strong>Water.</strong> Carry what the day needs, and know where you'll get more before you leave the truck. Planning your water on the mountain is planning it too late.</li>
        <li><strong>Emergency snacks.</strong> The calories you don't plan to eat. They ride at the bottom of the pack, trip after trip, until the day the trip goes sideways — and then they're the most important thing you packed. Don't raid them on a slow afternoon.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="panel group">
      <h2>Kill kit</h2>
      <ul class="check">
        <li><strong>Lighter.</strong> Yes, a second one — one lives in the med kit, one lives here. Redundancy isn't paranoia when the item weighs eleven grams and makes fire.</li>
        <li><strong>Gloves.</strong> Blood-soaked hands go numb fast in October, and clean meat care is easier when you're not rationing paper towels. Cheap, light, disposable.</li>
        <li><strong>Construction trash bag.</strong> Contractor-grade, not kitchen-grade — kitchen bags shred the first time bone or brush touches them. It's a meat barrier on dirt, a pack liner in rain, a makeshift rain layer, and a clean surface anywhere you need one.</li>
        <li><strong>Razor blades.</strong> Lighter than a spare knife, always surgically sharp, and they weigh nothing. When the real knife dulls halfway through a quartering job, a fresh blade finishes clean. Sharpening on the mountain is a skill; swapping a blade is a certainty.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p class="muted">
      Read the list again and the pattern shows itself: fire appears twice, in two different
      pockets. The sock, the tarp, and the trash bag each do three or four jobs. And the knife
      never leaves the body. That's the whole doctrine — weight goes to things that do two jobs
      or save your life. Everything else stays in the truck.
    </p>

    <EmailCTA />
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  section { margin-top: 3rem; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 6vw, 2.6rem); margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem; }
  .lede { color: var(--muted); font-size: 1.08rem; }
  .group { margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  .group h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .check { padding-left: 1.2rem; margin-bottom: 0; }
  .check li { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
  p.muted { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
</style>
